    id: "c5",
    slug: "1987-ferrari-f40",
    title: "1987 Ferrari F40: The Raw 200 MPH Carbon-Kevlar Icon",
    excerpt: "The last car approved by Enzo Ferrari himself—pure analog violence with twin turbos, gated manual, and zero driver aids.",
    body: "Built to celebrate Ferrari's 40th anniversary, the F40 was the final car personally approved by *Il Commendatore*, Enzo Ferrari, before his passing in 1988. Enzo's brief to chief engineer Nicola Materazzi was simple: build a no-compromise road-legal racing car with zero fluff.\n\nThere were no anti-lock brakes. No power steering. No traction control. No door handles (just pull cables). The green glue holding the carbon-Kevlar weave was visible on the bare cabin floor.\n\n![1987 Ferrari F40 Twin-Turbo Supercar in Rosso Corsa | Picture courtesy of Wikimedia Commons](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/F40_ferrari_2019.jpg/1280px-F40_ferrari_2019.jpg)\n\n## The Powertrain: Twin-Turbo Ferocity\n\n- **Engine:** 2.9-liter Tipo F120A 90° V8 with twin IHI turbochargers producing **478 HP at 7,000 rpm and 577 Nm of torque**.\n- **Weight:** Just 1,100 kg dry weight thanks to carbon fiber, Kevlar, and Nomex honeycomb composite construction.\n- **Top Speed:** 324 km/h (201 mph)—the first production road car officially verified over 200 mph.\n- **Transmission:** 5-speed gated manual with dog-leg first gear.\n\n> \"When the turbochargers spool past 4,000 rpm, the F40 explodes down the straight with terrifying, visceral savagery.\"\n\nThe F40 remains the definitive analog supercar against which all modern hypercars are measured.",
