    id: "c7",
    slug: "1970-dodge-challenger-rt-426-hemi",
    title: "1970 Dodge Challenger R/T 426 Hemi: Peak American Muscle",
    excerpt: "Equipped with dual four-barrel carburetors and shaker hood scoop, the 426 Hemi Challenger remains the gold standard of drag-strip fury.",
    body: "In 1970, the American muscle car horsepower wars reached their absolute boiling point. Chrysler entered the pony car market with the E-body Dodge Challenger R/T, offering an engine option that struck fear into drag strips nationwide: the legendary 426 Street Hemi.\n\nKnown as the 'Elephant Engine' for its massive physical dimensions and indestructible cast iron construction, the Hemi featured hemispherical combustion chambers with massive 2.25-inch intake valves.\n\n![1970 Dodge Challenger R/T 426 Hemi with Shaker Hood | Picture courtesy of Wikimedia Commons](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/1970_Dodge_Challenger_RT_426_Hemi.jpg/1280px-1970_Dodge_Challenger_RT_426_Hemi.jpg)\n\n## Street Hemi Specifications\n\n- **Engine:** 7.0-liter 426 Hemi V8 with dual Carter AFB four-barrel carburetors.\n- **Power:** Factory under-rated at **425 HP and 490 lb-ft of torque** (dyno tests frequently revealed over 475 hp).\n- **Shaker Hood Scoop:** Mounted directly to the air cleaner atop the carburetors, shaking with the engine's idle.\n- **Quarter-Mile:** 13.5 seconds on bias-ply street tires.\n\nOnly 356 Challenger R/T coupes were built with the 426 Hemi in 1970, making surviving original numbers-matching cars among the most valuable American vehicles on Earth.",
